<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Huguenots</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as French Protestants</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Huguenots were French Protestants of the Reformed, or Calvinist, tradition. They fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-wars-of-religion">French Wars of Religion</a> from 1562 and gained toleration under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edict-of-nantes">Edict of Nantes</a> in 1598. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> revoked it in 1685, some 200,000 fled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and overseas colonies, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope">Cape of Good Hope</a>, where about 200 settled from 1688.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/huguenots.jpg" alt="Huguenots" width="320" height="217" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Paul Landowski / Henri Bouchard · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ReformationsdenkmalGenf1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
